\usepackage[margin=25mm]{geometry}\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm}
\setlength{\emergencystretch}{2em}
\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}
\title{Eight pentagons through every edge}
\author{Human Compression project}\date{}
\begin{document}\maketitle
\begin{theorem}
There is no finite simple quartic graph of girth five in which every
edge lies in exactly eight pentagons. In the usual notation,
$n(4,5,8)=\infty$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Suppose such a graph exists and fix a vertex $v$. Let its neighbours
be $p_0,p_1,p_2,p_3$, and put $L_i=N(p_i)\setminus\{v\}$.
The absence of triangles and quadrangles implies that the $L_i$ are
disjoint triples. None has an internal edge. Write $H$ for the graph
induced by their twelve vertices. Between any two branches $L_i,L_j$
the edges form a matching: two neighbours in $L_j$ of a vertex in
$L_i$ would, together with $p_j$, form a quadrangle.
Let $m_{ij}\le3$ be the size of that matching.

Every pentagon through the edge $vp_i$ has the unique form
\[
                         v,p_i,x,y,p_j,v,
 \qquad x\in L_i,\quad y\in L_j,\quad j\ne i.
\]
Conversely, every such edge $xy$ supplies a pentagon. Therefore
$\sum_{j\ne i}m_{ij}=8$ for each $i$. The nonnegative integers
$d_{ij}=3-m_{ij}$ are symmetric and satisfy
$\sum_{j\ne i}d_{ij}=1$. The deficient pairs consequently form a
perfect matching on the four branch labels. Relabel so that
\[
 m_{01}=m_{23}=2,\qquad
 m_{02}=m_{03}=m_{12}=m_{13}=3.                         \tag{1}
\]

Retain only the four complete matchings in (1), obtaining a graph
$J$ on the twelve leaves. Each vertex has degree two in $J$.
Along a component, its branch labels follow the cyclic order
$0,2,1,3,0,2,1,3,\ldots$ or its reverse. Indeed, at each leaf the
two incident edges lead to the two different adjacent labels in
this four-cycle. Each component is thus a cycle of length divisible
by four. A component of length four is forbidden by the girth of
the original graph. On twelve vertices the only remaining possibility
is a single cycle of length twelve: a component of length eight
would leave four vertices in another component.

Number its vertices $x_0,\ldots,x_{11}$ in cyclic order. Each of the
four omitted edges of $H$ joins opposite labels of the four-cycle
on branch labels. Its endpoints therefore differ by $2$, $6$ or $10$
modulo twelve. Differences $2$ and $10$ would close a triangle with
two cycle edges. Every omitted edge must consequently be an antipodal
chord $x_r x_{r+6}$.

There are six possible antipodal chords, indexed by $r$ modulo six,
and four of them occur. A set of four residues modulo six contains
two consecutive residues: otherwise each chosen residue could be
paired with its distinct unchosen successor, leaving room for at
most three choices. If these consecutive residues are $r,r+1$,
their chords and the two cycle edges give the quadrangle
\[
                   x_r,x_{r+1},x_{r+7},x_{r+6},x_r,
\]
with indices read modulo twelve. Its four vertices are distinct.
This contradicts the assumed girth and proves the theorem.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Source comparison and attribution.}
Goedgebeur and Jooken, \emph{Exhaustive generation of edge-girth-regular
graphs}, arXiv:2401.08271v2, Table 2 on physical page 18, record
$n(4,5,8)\ge65$ and no finite upper bound. The displayed infinity
in their upper-bound column means that no example is reported; it
is not a proof of nonexistence. The theorem above implies their
finite lower bound and strengthens it to an all-order exclusion.
The argument uses only the root branches, their matching deficits,
and the cycle forced by four complete matchings. No earlier graph
classification or computational nonexistence result is a premise.

The supplementary certificate checks all $5184$ labelled matching
patterns after fixing the deficient branch pairs and the unmatched
leaf in each branch. Every pattern contains a triangle or quadrangle.
The proof above is independent of that enumeration. We claim neither
mathematical priority nor resolution of a separately formulated
published conjecture here; those questions require the subsequent
attribution audit. This component concerns one source row and gives
no whole-original, Lean or measured compression credit.
\end{document}
